---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const description = 'localdobe processes every PDF locally in your browser. No file ever reaches a server. Here is exactly what that means and what little data does exist.';
---
<Base title="Privacy — localdobe" {description} canonicalPath="/privacy">
  <article class="prose prose-slate mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pt-12">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>
      The short version: your files never leave your device. Every tool on localdobe —
      merge, split, compress, edit, watermark, protect, unlock, and signature validation —
      runs entirely in your browser using WebAssembly and JavaScript. When you drop a PDF
      into a tool, it's read into memory on your machine, processed there, and handed back
      to you as a download. At no point does it travel over the network to us or anyone else.
      You can verify this yourself: open your browser's network tab while using a tool, and
      you'll see no upload of file data.
    </p>
    <h2>What we don't do</h2>
    <p>
      We run no analytics of any kind — no Google Analytics, no Plausible, no tracking
      pixels, nothing that watches what you do on this site. We don't set cookies. We don't
      have accounts, so there's nothing to log in to and nothing tied to an identity.
    </p>
    <h2>What actually happens on the wire</h2>
    <p>
      The static site itself (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and the WebAssembly modules the tools
      use) is served by Cloudflare. Like any CDN, Cloudflare's edge network may log standard
      HTTP request metadata — the page URL you requested, your approximate location from IP,
      timestamp, and similar operational data — the same way any web server does. That
      logging covers which pages were requested, never the contents of any file you process.
      Your PDFs are not part of that traffic at all, because they never leave your browser.
    </p>
    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      localdobe is an independent project. We'd rather you verify these claims than take
      our word for them — its source code and a contact channel will be linked from this
      page once the repository is published.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
